Mark Healey wrote:

 > This is really a question about the patch program
 > but I'll need it to apply some patches so I'm
 > asking here.

 > I installed the source RPM for fedora 8.  It had
 > the tarball and some patches.  I extracted the
 > tarball and tried

 > ]patch nethack-3.4.3-config.patch

 > and got nothing, not even disk thrashing, until I
 > CTRL-Ced out of it.

That's because it was sitting waiting for you to
input the patch data via standard input, i.e., type
it in, since what you had done didn't feed it any
other data on standard input where patch was looking
to receive it.

 > What am I doing wrong?


well,

       $ man patch

gives in part:

       NAME
            patch - apply a diff file to an original

       SYNOPSIS
            patch [options] [origfile [patchfile]] [+  [options]  [orig-
            file]]...

            but usually just

            patch <patchfile

which, you'll notice, expects the first file on the
command line to be the original file of the
to-be-accomplished patch, not the file containing
the patches.

You really need to do the redirection shown in the
last line of the man page excerpt above to have
anything interesting happen.

Past that, patch is fairly tricky to use despite all
those who will tell you otherwise.

Your current working directory has to be the one
containing the (typically tree of) files to patch,
you may need to trim away some prefix parts of the
absolute or relative file-pathnames used within the
patch input file, and etc. It's really worth
studying the documentation for patch until you
understand it in great detail before trying to use
it to do anything particularly complicated, like
applying patches to a big software kit like that of
NetHack

